# Weirvern Zeek sensor image.
#
# WHY A DERIVED IMAGE: Zeek 9.0.0 core ships NO JA4 (or JA3) policy -- verified by
# grepping the entire /usr/local/zeek/share/zeek tree on the live pod: zero hits. TLS
# fingerprints now live in a package, and a package has to be installed at BUILD time.
#
# We install it here rather than running `zkg install` at container start because the
# deployment must not fetch anything at boot: a sensor that depends on a network fetch to
# come up will eventually fail to come up.
#
#     podman build -t registry.example.com/weirvern-zeek:0.4.0 -f zeek/Containerfile zeek/
#     podman push registry.example.com/weirvern-zeek:0.4.0
#
#     cd k8s && kustomize edit set image weirvern-zeek=registry.example.com/weirvern-zeek:0.4.0
#
# ── LICENSING: JA4+ IS REMOVED FROM THIS IMAGE, NOT DISABLED ─────────────────────
#
# The upstream package is split-licensed, and the two halves are NOT interchangeable:
#
#   JA4  (TLS client fingerprint)  BSD-3-Clause        free for any use incl. commercial
#   JA4+ (JA4S/H/X/SSH/T/L/D)      FoxIO License 1.1   internal/academic only;
#                                                     NOT for monetization
#
# The FoxIO FAQ states that a company which "uses JA4+ to provide value to paying
# customers, even without exposing JA4+ fingerprints directly to those customers, ...
# would still need an OEM license as they are indirectly monetizing JA4+", and that the
# JA4+ methods are patent pending.
#
# So Weirvern ships JA4 ONLY -- and the FoxIO submodules are DELETED from the filesystem
# while the image is built (see the prune step below), rather than being switched off by
# options at load time.
#
# WHY DELETION RATHER THAN DISABLING. Option-based disabling works, but it is one flag
# away from being wrong, and it has already been wrong here once in a way nothing
# detected:
#
#   the package gates submodules with `@if (JA4S_enabled) @load ./ja4s @endif`, and
#   `@if` is evaluated WHEN THE FILE IS LOADED, not when the option changes. Disabling
#   after loading was silently ignored -- measured: 28 of 28 ssl records carried a
#   populated ja4s while the config claimed it was off.
#
# That was fixable by reordering the loads, and it was fixed. But it demonstrates the
# class of failure: the licensed code sat in the image, and only the correctness of a
# load order stood between it and shipping. Deleting the files removes the question.
# Nothing to mis-order, nothing a package update can re-enable by changing a default,
# and no licensed source in the artefact we distribute.
#
# The prune is verified at BUILD time by assertions that FAIL THE BUILD if any FoxIO
# submodule, any FoxIO option, or any FoxIO-licensed output field survives. A licensing
# guarantee that depends on a human reading a comment is not a guarantee.
#
# We therefore do NOT use the third-party `anthonykasza/ja4` (pure BSD-3-Clause, which
# would be a cleaner licensing position) because it FAILS ITS OWN UNIT TESTS on Zeek
# 9.0.0 and cannot be installed: `zkg install` aborts with "test_command failed". A
# fingerprint that is subtly wrong is worse than none, since it silently fails to match
# threat-intel data.
FROM zeek/zeek:lts

# zkg needs a writable config dir and must not try to be interactive. The image already
# ships zkg, cmake, and the Zeek headers are NOT present, so only scripts-only packages can
# be installed here -- a C++ plugin would fail to build. That is fine: ja4-zeek-scripts is
# the scripts-only variant, deliberately.
#
# ZKG_HOME only. Do NOT set ZEEKPATH here: the upstream image deliberately leaves it
# unset, and pinning it to share/zeek breaks discovery of the builtin plugins under
# lib/zeek/plugins. That failure is loud but obscure --
#   "fatal error in builtin-plugins/__load__.zeek: can't find Zeek_JavaScript/__load__.zeek"
# -- and it takes the whole sensor down. Verified by comparing the two images: upstream
# ZEEKPATH is empty, mine was /usr/local/zeek/share/zeek, and mine crashed at startup.
# `zkg` finds its own paths without help; nothing here needs ZEEKPATH.
ENV ZKG_HOME=/usr/local/zeek/var/lib/zkg

# Install the package. Two notes:
#
#  * `zkg install` writes into /usr/local/zeek/share/zeek/site/packages and appends a load
#    to site/local.zeek. Our site policy is mounted from a ConfigMap at runtime and is
#    passed as a bare path on the command line, so it does not inherit that local.zeek --
#    the policy @loads the package explicitly instead. That keeps the licensing controls
#    in one reviewable file rather than split between image and ConfigMap.
#
#  * There is no --noload flag (verified: zkg rejects it). Loading at install time is
#    harmless here because the runtime policy is what decides which submodules load, and
#    the FoxIO ones are deleted in the next step regardless.
#
# `set -e` semantics matter: an earlier version chained these with `&&`/`||` and the
# trailing `||` masked a failure in the INSTALL itself, so the build reported a generic
# message instead of zkg's actual error. Each step is a separate RUN so a failure names
# the step that failed.
# The prune script is copied in BEFORE the step that runs it.
COPY prune-ja4plus.sh /tmp/prune-ja4plus.sh
COPY verify-ja4.sh /tmp/verify-ja4.sh

RUN zkg install --force foxio/ja4-zeek-scripts

# Fail the build if the package did not land where the site policy expects it. Without
# this, a silently-skipped install produces an image that builds fine and whose sensor
# then aborts at startup with "failed to locate script".
RUN test -f /usr/local/zeek/share/zeek/site/packages/ja4-zeek-scripts/config.zeek \
 || { echo "ERROR: the ja4 package is not present at the expected path"; exit 1; }

# ── PRUNE JA4+ : DELETE the FoxIO-licensed submodules ───────────────────────────
#
# The mechanism lives in prune-ja4plus.sh rather than inline here, so it can be run against
# a plain directory and asserted in the test suite -- a licensing guard that can only be
# exercised by a full container build is a guard that will not be exercised.
#
# It deletes the seven FoxIO submodule directories, deletes the package's own tests (which
# carry recorded JA4+ OUTPUT, e.g. tests/Traces/Scripts.ja4-ssh2/ja4ssh.log), rewrites the
# loader so it cannot reference them, filters the FoxIO options out of config.zeek, and then
# FAILS THE BUILD if any of that did not take. It also asserts JA4's own options SURVIVED --
# an over-eager filter would leave a sensor that parses and produces no fingerprint at all,
# which is silently worse than a loud build failure.
RUN sh /tmp/prune-ja4plus.sh \
        /usr/local/zeek/share/zeek/site/packages/ja4-zeek-scripts

# Prove the remaining policy parses and that the connection record is intact. A `zeek -e`
# parse needs no capture interface.
RUN zeek -e 'exit(0)' 2>&1 | head -3 ; true

# Prove at BUILD time that the pruned package still LOADS. Without this, the prune could
# leave a package that parses but produces no fingerprint -- a silently useless sensor, which
# is the failure this whole image exists to avoid.
#
# THIS CHECK IS A SCRIPT, NOT AN INLINE HEREDOC. The first version wrote local.zeek with an
# inline `cat > file <<'ZEEK'` inside a line-continued RUN, which Dockerfile syntax does not
# support -- the build failed with "unterminated heredoc". Moving it to the same script that
# does the pruning keeps the mechanism testable outside a container as well.
RUN sh /tmp/verify-ja4.sh \
        /usr/local/zeek/share/zeek/site/packages/ja4-zeek-scripts

# Record WHY we ship this package, what its license split is, and that JA4+ was REMOVED,
# inside the image, so an operator inspecting a running sensor can see it without reading
# our repo.
COPY JA4-NOTICE /usr/local/zeek/JA4-NOTICE

# The site policy is mounted from a ConfigMap at runtime (see k8s/32-zeek.yaml), not baked
# in, so it can be changed without rebuilding this image. It does NOT need to disable the
# FoxIO submodules any more -- they are not in this image.
